WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
Lovesick, head over heels,
butterflies in the tummy.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.360
We all have ways of describing
what love feels like,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.240
but what is actually going on
in our brains when we fall in love?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.080
And how do we know how to distinguish
lust from love?

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.320
According to Helen E Fisher,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.760
from Rutgers University,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.360
there are three aspects
of romantic love.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.600
Often lust comes first,
but not always.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.680
For some people who are asexual,
it may not happen at all.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.960
But for those who do experience lust,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.080
it's driven by the hormones
oestrogen and testosterone.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.040
They affect our libido,
the sex drive.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.160
It may feel purely carnal, but
in fact it's about the urge to mate

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.240
and pass on your DNA via offspring.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.960
Without lust, it's fair to say
our species would not survive.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.160
The second aspect of romantic love
is attraction,

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.600
influenced by a neurotransmitter
called dopamine.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.640
This is a feel-good substance
released in our brain

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.800
that is involved in driving us
towards reward.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.040
Do something, get a dopamine hit,
feel good.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.240
Eventually dopamine will push us
toward repeating that behaviour.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:22.920
This is why intense attraction

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.400
feels like an addiction
to another human being.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.160
Some people get stuck in that loop,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.120
always chasing the
dopamine-soaked excitement

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.440
of a new relationship.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.240
Might as well face it -
you're addicted to love.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.400
Dopamine also dampens
the region of the brain

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.000
that regulates critical thinking
and rational behaviour.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.760
People often stay in this somewhat
irrational honeymoon period

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.480
for up to 18 months.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.680
Another hormone that plays a role
in attraction is norepinephrine

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.360
which is involved in generating
the physiological responses

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.360
we experience in this state.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.760
Think sweaty palms, heart fluttering,
breathing faster.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.720
In that sense, strong attraction
is similar to a state of stress,

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.600
but it's good stress,

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.760
because we associate these sensations
with positive things

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.120
such as the sight, sound,
or even the smell of our lover.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.240
The third aspect is attachment
or bonding.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.240
There are two other hormones at work
here, oxytocin and vasopressin.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.640
Oxytocin, known as
the cuddle hormone,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.720
is released during sex
and skin-to-skin contact.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.440
This heightens feelings of security
and contentment

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.120
which help forge deep bonds
with a mate.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.680
In some people, negative
experiences as a child

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.880
can affect the oxytocin system

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.680
making it harder for them
to form attachments as adults.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.400
But thankfully, with the right kind
of therapy, our brains can be rewired

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.000
to form healthy relationships.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.160
As for vasopressin, this is released
directly after having sex

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.760
and it too produces feelings
of contentment.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.840
This may have a taming effect
on more promiscuous people

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.480
by triggering the
neural reward system,

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.720
encouraging you to want to stay
with the person you love.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.680
And all being well, with time,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.760
love can morph into something
stable and fulfilling.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.960
Research has shown that kindness
plays a key role.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.640
You could say that a long-term bond
is a continuous exercise in kindness.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.440
Of course, love can also
have a darker side.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.240
Obsessive, jealous behaviours
can be intensified

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.520
by falling levels of serotonin,
a mood-levelling hormone.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.880
And we all know that love
doesn't always last,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.240
and when it doesn't, it hurts.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.080
Heartbreak is real.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.640
It's known as broken heart syndrome

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.120
and can even be mistaken
for a heart attack.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.280
The emotional stress that
accompanies break-ups

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.640
releases chemicals that usually
signal physical pain,

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.480
so our brain interprets the break-up
as intensely painful.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.600
But despite the heartbreaks,
the sweaty palms,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.560
and the irrational behaviour,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.840
people fall in love and stay in love

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.120
every minute of every day.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.400
Because those dopamine hits
can feel amazing.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.120
So even if it doesn't always
feel like it,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.120
the head really does rule the heart.

